//! Lava's own look: a shared ground effect for Earthquake's fissure and Volcano's flow (`LavaView`, below,
//! draws `game_core::lava`'s `EffectKind::Lava`; pop3-rev-analysis.md "Lava": hot cells ignite people,
//! buildings and trees, sparing only the caster's own shaman). Procedural (seamless, tileable, no original art
//! to trace: the original has no lava tile in its blend tables, docs/specs/terrain-textures.md), generated by
//! pure functions, no Bevy, down to `generate`, like `procedural_theme`/`terrain_texture`.
//!
//! The original's own draw path is traced in full (pop3-rev-analysis.md "Building ashes", which is about the
//! shared blend-byte mechanism, not ashes specifically): every map cell has one blend byte (`+0xA`, our own
//! `blend`, 0-255), and the terrain renderer samples **that one cell's own byte directly, no averaging with
//! neighbours**, as a vertex colour at the cell's own grid position, then lets ordinary bilinear interpolation
//! blend it into its neighbours across each quad — "this, not any lava-specific effect, is why adjoining hot
//! cells visually read as a soft round blob per cell that fuses into a connected glow where two hot cells share
//! an edge: a single hot cell peaks at its own corners and fades toward cold neighbours, while two adjacent hot
//! cells keep their shared edge bright, with no dip between them." `ground_mesh_grid`/`Vertex` below rebuild
//! exactly that: one `Vertex` per cell, looked up at its own grid position by every quad that touches it (shared,
//! not duplicated per-cell data) — this alone is what used to read as a hard-edged checkerboard (one flat quad
//! per lit cell, each cell's own alpha applied uniformly to all 4 of its own corners) now reads as merged,
//! organic blobs, closing the gaps the simulation's own single-direction spread (`game_core::lava::grow`) leaves
//! between cells without drawing anything extra there: a "gap" cell simply has no `Vertex` of its own, so its
//! quad's corners fall back to its lit neighbours' values and the interpolation does the rest.
//!
//! The same section is explicit that **the "brighter, then fading" look is nothing but that one blend byte
//! driving a 64-row colour-table lookup every frame — "no animation exists in this draw path or the palette
//! table itself... not a separate pulsing/flicker effect layered on top"**: ash itself is a fixed, dark row
//! (31, blend 127), the 128-255 range (rows 32-63) is lava-exclusive and "presumably glowing", and a cell's own
//! blend rises into that range as it matures and spreads, falls back out of it over its fade, then settles
//! permanently at the ash row. `glow_tint` reproduces that arc directly off `blend` alone (dark and ashen below
//! the 127 crossover, brightening to an overexposed glow above it) as a vertex-colour multiplier on the
//! procedural texture — the original has no transparency in this path at all, it repaints the opaque terrain
//! texture outright, so we drape a separate blended mesh instead and still need an alpha of our own
//! (`arrival_alpha`, `fade_alpha`): a quick fade-in on arrival (our own cushion against a translucent quad
//! popping in, which the original's opaque repaint never had), and a fade-out keyed off
//! `game_core::lava::Lava::fade_progress` once a flowing patch enters its own fixed-length cooling fade — a
//! cell's `blend` alone isn't enough for that (it only ever drops by a fixed total over that fade, so a cell that
//! grew hot enough can still read as nearly full strength the turn before it abruptly turns to permanent ash) —
//! so the whole patch visibly fades away in step with that same countdown, handing off to the ash mark
//! underneath (`ground.wgsl`'s own ash-charring step, blended the same organic way, docs/specs/terrain-textures.md
//! "Our bake") instead of popping.

